WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.890
Open Synthetic Reporter and select
Synthetic Reporter DNA.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.590
Use the block's Actions menu and choose
Design primers.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.670
The dialog calculates candidate pairs for
the selected target.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.830
Check the target coordinates first.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.030
This example uses bases fifty-one through
five hundred fifty.

6
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.860
The primer length range is eighteen to
twenty-eight bases, and the target

7
00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.950
melting temperature is sixty degrees
Celsius.

8
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.170
These settings appear above the ranked
results.

9
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.745
Read Pair one. Its forward and reverse
sequences are shown in the

10
00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:37.750
five-prime to three-prime direction.
Compare their melting temperatures

11
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.710
and GC percentages, and check the predicted
amplicon length.

12
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.870
This pair produces a five hundred fourteen
base-pair amplicon.

13
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.150
Click Add selected pair. The dialog closes
after adding the annotations.

14
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.670
Open Features in the Inspector to verify
the result.

15
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.720
The source DNA now includes a named forward
primer and reverse primer

16
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.770
alongside its existing annotations. You can
use those feature names and

17
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.164
coordinates to revisit the selected pair.
